aionnich/docs/Tutorial.md:3:SimAI is a comprehensive large-scale AI training simulation toolkit that provides three major simulation scenarios:
aionnich/docs/Tutorial.md:7:    * *Performance Analysis*: Compare completion times across different models (e.g., studying the impact of Expert numbers on MoE model training performance)
aionnich/docs/Tutorial.md:15:> 💡 *Currently supports manual busbw.yaml configuration. Automatic busbw inference based on parallel scenarios will be open-sourced soon. Stay tuned and feel free to contact us for more details. ✨*
aionnich/docs/Tutorial.md:17:2. **SimAI-Simulation(NS-3)** - A high-fidelity, full-stack simulation tool that can theoretically integrate with any pure network simulator. It provides fine-grained reproduction of communication behaviors during LLM training. Currently supports NS-3 as the network backend (we encourage integration of new network simulation tools). Key research areas include:
aionnich/docs/Tutorial.md:33:Under normal circumstances, running SimAI requires generating a Workload file using the [AICB](https://github.com/aliyun/aicb?tab=readme-ov-file#generate-workloads-for-simulation-simai) tool. To create a precise Workload, you may need to utilize the AIOB feature to determine the timing of various computational kernels, which necessitates a GPU environment. Therefore, we recommend executing the SimAI full-stack toolkit directly within the latest **NGC image**.
aionnich/docs/Tutorial.md:60:## 📝 Workload Generate
aionnich/docs/Tutorial.md:62:To generate workloads for simulation, use the [SimAI-WorkloadGenerator](https://github.com/aliyun/aicb?tab=readme-ov-file#generate-workloads-for-simulation-simai) feature in [AICB](https://github.com/aliyun/aicb). This will produce a `.txt` file similar to [workload_analytical.txt](../example/workload_analytical.txt), which includes:
aionnich/docs/Tutorial.md:64:- `model_parallel_NPU_group`: Represents the size of Tensor Parallelism
aionnich/docs/Tutorial.md:67:- `vpp`: Virtual Pipeline Parallelism (default: `--num-layers-per-virtual-pipeline-stage=1` for minimal PP bubble)
aionnich/docs/Tutorial.md:69:> 💡 *For more details, refer to the [AICB Workload Tutorial](https://github.com/aliyun/aicb/blob/master/training/tutorial.md#workload)*
aionnich/docs/Tutorial.md:100:$ ./bin/SimAI_analytical -w example/workload_analytical.txt -g 9216 -g_p_s 8 -r test- -busbw example/busbw.yaml
aionnich/docs/Tutorial.md:107:| `-w` | `--workload` | Specifies the path to the Workload File |
aionnich/docs/Tutorial.md:119:### Communication Group Overlap Ratios
aionnich/docs/Tutorial.md:121:The following parameters specify the overlap ratios for communication groups (default: 0, indicating no overlap):
aionnich/docs/Tutorial.md:125:| `-dp_o` | `--dp-overlap-ratio` | DP overlap ratio | [0.0-1.0] |
aionnich/docs/Tutorial.md:126:| `-ep_o` | `--ep-overlap-ratio` | EP overlap ratio | [0.0-1.0] |
aionnich/docs/Tutorial.md:127:| `-tp_o` | `--tp-overlap-ratio` | TP overlap ratio | [0.0-1.0] |
aionnich/docs/Tutorial.md:128:| `-pp_o` | `--pp-overlap-ratio` | PP overlap ratio | [0.0-1.0] |
aionnich/docs/Tutorial.md:130:> 📝 *Due to the variety of overlap strategies and scenario-dependent overlap ratios, we prioritize simple and efficient methods to directly specify overlap conditions.*
